00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.680
Now speaking of changing climate conditions and their effect on contaminant behavior,

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.780
here's something worth carrying into your next interface project. You landed a northern

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:38.200
British Columbia interface subdivision two days after a wildfire slept through the night,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:43.800
but reignited at two meters, jumping the control line and destroying three homes. The incident

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:49.520
commanders report notes that overnight relative humidity failed to recover above 35%.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.680
That is exactly the threshold where Canadian forest fire weather index system predictions begin

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.860
to diverge from observed fire behavior. Under historic climate normals forests experience roughly

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:06.560
550 fewer fire-friendly hours per year in many western provinces. Current projections show those hours

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:11.460
increasing, compressing the safe suppression window and altering liability calculations for both

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:17.160
private consultants and provincial wildfire agencies. When preparing predesturbance risk assessments

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:23.280
for oil and gas or mining clients near the wildland urban interface, experience practitioners now run

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.140
fire weather index scenarios through downscaled climate ensembles rather than relying on 30-year

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:35.820
normals. Then overlay those outputs on contaminant transport models because fire-induced soil hydrophobicity

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:42.220
can mobilize previously stable hydrocarbons or metals. The nuance most juniors miss is that nocturnal

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:48.760
fire spread is no longer an anomaly, but the new baseline in many jurisdictions. This requires updated

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:54.440
emergency response plans that integrate real-time fuel moisture probes instead of solely depending on

00:08:54.440 --> 00:09:00.960
distant weather stations. The most common mistake is submitting a wildfire mitigation plan based on

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:07.240
daytime only burn windows. The fix is to require overnight fire behavior modeling using at least two

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:12.440
independent climate projections and to explicitly address liability allocation for after-dark

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:17.640
